<%= render "shared/page_header",
      title: "Elephant Family Tree",
      intro: "Scientists sort living things into groups, a bit like sorting toys into boxes. This is called classification. " \
             "Let's find out where elephants fit!" %>

<div class="section">
  <h2>How scientists sort elephants</h2>
  <p>Each group is smaller than the one before it. Scientists give every group a Latin name.</p>
  <table class="data-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Group</th>
        <th>Science name</th>
        <th>What it means</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <% [
        ["Kingdom", "Animalia", "All animals"],
        ["Phylum", "Chordata", "Animals with a spinal cord (a bundle of nerves down the back), like fish, birds and you"],
        ["Class", "Mammalia", "Mammals: animals with hair that feed their babies milk"],
        ["Order", "Proboscidea", "Animals with a trunk"],
        ["Family", "Elephantidae", "Elephants and mammoths"],
        ["Genus", "Loxodonta and Elephas", "African elephants and Asian elephants"]
      ].each do |rank, name, meaning| %>
        <tr>
          <th><%= rank %></th>
          <td><em><%= name %></em></td>
          <td><%= meaning %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>Three kinds of elephants</h2>
  <p>
    There are three species (kinds) of elephant alive today. All three are <strong>endangered</strong>. That means so few
    are left that they could disappear forever.
  </p>
  <div class="card-grid">
    <div class="card" data-mascot-fact="A savanna elephant&#x27;s ears can measure about 1.5 metres from top to bottom.">
      <span class="section-label"><em>Loxodonta africana</em></span>
      <h3>African savanna elephant</h3>
      <p>
        The biggest animal on land! Its huge ears are shaped a bit like a map of Africa. Males and females both grow tusks.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card" data-mascot-fact="Forest elephants spread the seeds of so many trees that scientists call them &#x27;megagardeners&#x27; of the forest.">
      <span class="section-label"><em>Loxodonta cyclotis</em></span>
      <h3>African forest elephant</h3>
      <p>
        Smaller, with rounder ears and straight tusks that point down. This helps it squeeze through thick forest.
        It was only listed as its own species in 2021, and it's in the most danger of all.
      </p>
    </div>
    <div class="card" data-mascot-fact="Asian elephants have worked alongside people for over 4,000 years.">
      <span class="section-label"><em>Elephas maximus</em></span>
      <h3>Asian elephant</h3>
      <p>
        Smaller ears, a rounded back and two bumps on top of its head. Most females have no tusks, and many males don't either.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>Kinds of Asian elephant</h2>
  <p>
    Scientists split Asian elephants into smaller groups called subspecies. Each one lives in a different place.
    (African elephants aren't split up like this.)
  </p>
  <ul class="fact-list">
    <li data-mascot-fact="About 27,000 wild elephants live in India, around 60% of all wild Asian elephants.">
      <span class="fact-label">Indian elephant<br><em class="font-normal">E. m. indicus</em></span>
      <span class="fact-value">Lives in the most places, from India and Nepal all the way to Vietnam and Malaysia.</span>
    </li>
    <li data-mascot-fact="Only about 7% of male Sri Lankan elephants grow tusks.">
      <span class="fact-label">Sri Lankan elephant<br><em class="font-normal">E. m. maximus</em></span>
      <span class="fact-value">The biggest Asian elephants. Many have pink, freckly patches on their face and ears.</span>
    </li>
    <li data-mascot-fact="Sumatran elephants have one more pair of ribs than other Asian elephants: 20 pairs instead of 19.">
      <span class="fact-label">Sumatran elephant<br><em class="font-normal">E. m. sumatranus</em></span>
      <span class="fact-value">The smallest kind. It lives only on the island of Sumatra in Indonesia, and it's in great danger.</span>
    </li>
    <li data-mascot-fact="Bornean elephants have babyish faces, large ears and long tails that can almost touch the ground.">
      <span class="fact-label">Bornean elephant<br><em class="font-normal">E. m. borneensis</em></span>
      <span class="fact-value">Sometimes called the "pygmy elephant". It lives on the island of Borneo. Scientists still aren't sure if it's its own subspecies.</span>
    </li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="section">
  <h2>More classification details</h2>
  <div class="info-block" data-mascot-fact="The earliest relatives of elephants lived around 60 million years ago and were smaller than a dog.">
    <p><strong>Proboscidea</strong> comes from the Greek word for "trunk". The order once had dozens of species, including woolly mammoths and mastodons.</p>
    <p><strong>Loxodonta</strong> means "slanted tooth", after the lozenge-shaped ridges on African elephants' molars.</p>
    <p>The closest living relatives of elephants are <strong>manatees, dugongs and hyraxes</strong>.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="highlight-box" data-mascot-fact="Woolly mammoths survived on Wrangel Island until about 4,000 years ago, after the Great Pyramid of Giza was built!">
    <p><strong>Surprising fact:</strong> DNA studies show that the Asian elephant is more closely related to the extinct woolly mammoth than to African elephants.</p>
  </div>
</div>
